# Container Image Build Wrapper — Design & Implementation Guide

**Status:** Draft v1.0
**Owner:** Platform / DevOps team
**Audience:** Platform engineers implementing the wrapper; app teams consuming it
**Context:** Migration off Kaniko (archived Jan 2025). Current backend: Docker BuildKit (`docker buildx`). The wrapper exists so the backend can be replaced again in the future without touching application pipelines.

---

## 1. Goal and Principles

### 1.1 Problem

Today, every project pipeline calls the build tool directly. When the tool changes (Kaniko → BuildKit → whatever comes next), every repository must be edited, tested, and re-released. That is organization-wide technical debt.

### 1.2 Solution

Introduce a **build contract** owned by the platform team:

> "Given a build context and an image name, produce an OCI image in the registry and report its digest."

Application pipelines depend only on this contract. The tool that fulfills it (today BuildKit) is an internal implementation detail, hidden behind:

1. A **wrapper script** (`build-image.sh`) — the only place that knows buildx flags.
2. A **versioned builder image** that packages the script and its tooling.
3. A **shared CI template** that projects include — the only thing project pipelines reference.

A future tool swap = change in **one repository**, rolled out by a version bump.

### 1.3 Design principles

| Principle | Meaning |
|---|---|
| Contract over tool | Inputs/outputs are named after build *needs* (tags, args, platforms), never after tool flags. |
| No escape hatches | No `EXTRA_BUILDX_FLAGS`-style passthrough. New needs become new named inputs. |
| OCI is the boundary | The wrapper's only promise is a standard OCI image + digest. Every realistic future builder can fulfill it. |
| Versioned contract | Builder image is semver'd. Projects pin a major version. |
| Tested contract | A sample app in the wrapper repo proves the contract on every change. |

