## 4. Repository 1: `platform/image-builder`

### 4.1 Layout

```
image-builder/
├── Dockerfile              # the builder image itself
├── bin/
│   └── build-image.sh      # the wrapper (the ONLY place that knows buildx)
├── tests/
│   ├── sample-app/         # tiny app used by contract tests
│   │   ├── Dockerfile
│   │   └── main.go
│   └── contract-test.sh
├── .gitlab-ci.yml          # builds, tests, and releases the builder image
├── CHANGELOG.md
└── README.md               # contract documentation for consumers
```

### 4.2 `bin/build-image.sh`

```bash
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# build-image.sh — org-standard container image build (contract v1)
# Backend: BuildKit via docker buildx. This file is the ONLY place
# allowed to reference buildx. See README.md for the contract.
set -euo pipefail

# ---------- contract inputs ----------
: "${IMAGE_NAME:?IMAGE_NAME is required (e.g. registry.company.com/team/app)}"
IMAGE_TAGS=${IMAGE_TAGS:-${CI_COMMIT_SHA:?IMAGE_TAGS or CI_COMMIT_SHA required}}
DOCKERFILE=${DOCKERFILE:-Dockerfile}
BUILD_CONTEXT=${BUILD_CONTEXT:-.}
BUILD_ARGS=${BUILD_ARGS:-}
BUILD_SECRETS=${BUILD_SECRETS:-}
PLATFORMS=${PLATFORMS:-linux/amd64}
CACHE_REF=${CACHE_REF:-${IMAGE_NAME}:buildcache}
PUSH=${PUSH:-true}
LABELS=${LABELS:-}
TARGET=${TARGET:-}

# ---------- assemble flags ----------
flags=()

for t in $IMAGE_TAGS;     do flags+=(-t "${IMAGE_NAME}:${t}"); done
for a in $BUILD_ARGS;     do flags+=(--build-arg "$a"); done
for s in $BUILD_SECRETS;  do flags+=(--secret "id=${s%%=*},src=${s#*=}"); done
for l in $LABELS;         do flags+=(--label "$l"); done
[ -n "$TARGET" ] && flags+=(--target "$TARGET")

flags+=(-f "$DOCKERFILE")
flags+=(--platform "$PLATFORMS")
flags+=(--cache-from "type=registry,ref=${CACHE_REF}")
flags+=(--cache-to "type=registry,ref=${CACHE_REF},mode=max,image-manifest=true")
flags+=(--provenance=false)   # flip to true once registry + consumers support attestations

if [ "$PUSH" = "true" ]; then
  flags+=(--push)
else
  # still produce an image archive so PUSH=false builds are verifiable
  flags+=(--output "type=oci,dest=/tmp/image.oci")
fi

meta=/tmp/build-meta.json
flags+=(--metadata-file "$meta")

# ---------- ensure builder exists ----------
# BUILDKIT_ADDR is set by the CI template when using a remote/rootless buildkitd.
if [ -n "${BUILDKIT_ADDR:-}" ]; then
  docker buildx create --name ci --driver remote "$BUILDKIT_ADDR" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
  docker buildx use ci
fi

echo ">> build-image (contract v1, backend: buildkit)"
echo ">> image=${IMAGE_NAME} tags=[${IMAGE_TAGS}] platforms=${PLATFORMS} push=${PUSH}"

docker buildx build "${flags[@]}" "$BUILD_CONTEXT"

# ---------- contract outputs ----------
if [ "$PUSH" = "true" ]; then
  digest=$(jq -r '."containerimage.digest"' "$meta")
  [ -n "$digest" ] && [ "$digest" != "null" ] || { echo "ERROR: no digest in metadata"; exit 1; }
  echo "$digest" > image.digest
  echo "${IMAGE_NAME}@${digest}" > image.ref
  echo ">> pushed ${IMAGE_NAME}@${digest}"
fi
```

Notes:

- `--metadata-file` + `jq` produces the digest without re-querying the registry.
- `BUILDKIT_ADDR` keeps the script identical whether the runner has a local daemon or talks to a remote rootless `buildkitd` (Kubernetes case, §7).
- Registry login is handled by the CI template before the script runs (§5), keeping auth concerns out of the contract.

### 4.3 `Dockerfile` (the builder image)

```dockerfile
FROM docker:27-cli

RUN apk add --no-cache bash jq curl git \
 && mkdir -p /usr/lib/docker/cli-plugins \
 && BUILDX_VER=v0.21.2 \
 && curl -fsSL -o /usr/lib/docker/cli-plugins/docker-buildx \
      "https://github.com/docker/buildx/releases/download/${BUILDX_VER}/buildx-${BUILDX_VER}.linux-amd64" \
 && chmod +x /usr/lib/docker/cli-plugins/docker-buildx

COPY bin/build-image.sh /usr/local/bin/build-image.sh
RUN chmod +x /usr/local/bin/build-image.sh

ENTRYPOINT []
CMD ["build-image.sh"]
```

### 4.4 Versioning & release rules

Semantic versioning of the **contract**, published as image tags `X.Y.Z`, `X.Y`, and `X` (moving):

| Change | Bump |
|---|---|
| New optional input / output | minor |
| Bug fix, backend upgrade with identical behavior | patch |
| Removed/renamed input, changed default, changed output format | **major** |
| **Swapping the backend tool** while honoring the contract | minor or patch ✅ |

That last row is the whole point: replacing BuildKit later is *not* a breaking change for consumers if the contract holds.

Release flow in `image-builder/.gitlab-ci.yml`: build image → run contract tests (§8) → on git tag `vX.Y.Z`, push `:X.Y.Z`, retag `:X.Y` and `:X`.

