## 7. Backend Setup: Rootless BuildKit on Kubernetes Runners

(Skip this section if runners are VMs with a local Docker daemon — the same wrapper works unchanged because `BUILDKIT_ADDR` is simply unset.)

Deploy a shared rootless `buildkitd` in the cluster:

```yaml
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: buildkitd
  namespace: ci-build
spec:
  replicas: 2
  selector: { matchLabels: { app: buildkitd } }
  template:
    metadata:
      labels: { app: buildkitd }
      annotations:
        container.apparmor.security.beta.kubernetes.io/buildkitd: unconfined
    spec:
      containers:
        - name: buildkitd
          image: moby/buildkit:v0.20.2-rootless
          args:
            - --addr=tcp://0.0.0.0:1234
            - --oci-worker-no-process-sandbox
          securityContext:
            seccompProfile: { type: Unconfined }
            runAsUser: 1000
            runAsGroup: 1000
          readinessProbe:
            exec: { command: ["buildctl", "debug", "workers"] }
          resources:
            requests: { cpu: "1", memory: 2Gi }
            limits:   { cpu: "4", memory: 8Gi }
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: buildkitd
  namespace: ci-build
spec:
  selector: { app: buildkitd }
  ports: [{ port: 1234, targetPort: 1234 }]
```

Hardening to add before production: mTLS between jobs and buildkitd (`--tlscacert/--tlscert/--tlskey` on both sides), a NetworkPolicy restricting ingress to runner pods, and resource quotas. Alternative topology: per-job buildkitd sidecar (stronger isolation, no shared local cache — registry cache still works).

The CI template's `BUILDKIT_ADDR` points at this Service; the wrapper script needs no change either way. **This deployment is also an implementation detail** — replacing it later doesn't touch the contract.

